{"context":"Anvil orchestrates many kinds of work: packaging artifacts, running verification checks, activating releases, executing build steps, and performing framework-specific operations. Each of these workflows needs to coordinate multiple steps in a defined order, handle failures, and report results.\n\nWithout a consistent execution model, each workflow invents its own orchestration:\n\n**Embedded orchestration.** Workflow logic is embedded inside commands. A command that packages an artifact also decides what order to run verification checks. A command that activates a release also decides when to run migration phases. Orchestration and implementation become coupled, making both harder to change independently.\n\n**Script-driven orchestration.** Each workflow is a shell script that calls Anvil comman Source: ADR-007-pipeline-execution-model.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:Accepted.","decision":"Anvil adopts a task-oriented execution Pipeline.\n\nA Pipeline is an execution orchestration model. It is NOT a CI/CD workflow, though CI/CD workflows MAY invoke Pipelines.\n\nThe model has two fundamental roles:\n\n- **Pipeline** orchestrates execution. It decides what runs, in what order, and under what conditions.\n- **Task** performs execution. It runs a single unit of work and reports the result.\n\nPipelines know about ordering, dependencies, and execution flow. Tasks know about the specific work to be done.\n\nThe Pipeline contains NO framework-specific logic and NO business logic. It orchestrates execution of tasks that are defined externally through configuration.","consequences":"### 11.1 Positive Consequences\n\n**Reusable execution engine.** The Pipeline model is used for all execution — packaging, verification, activation, rollback, custom workflows. Every workflow benefits from the same execution semantics, failure handling, and observability.\n\n**Portable workflows.** Pipelines are defined in configuration, not in code or CI platform YAML. The same Pipeline definition works locally, in CI, and on production servers. Workflows are portable across environments.\n\n**Framework independence.** Pipelines orchestrate Tasks. Tasks may invoke framework-specific tools, but the Pipeline itself contains no framework logic. The execution model is framework-agnostic.\n\n**Predictable orchestration.** Every Pipeline follows the same execution rules. Operators know how any Pipeline","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n### 12.1 Framework-Controlled Execution\n\n**Description:** Each application framework defines its own execution model. Anvil adapts to whatever orchestration approach the framework provides.\n\n**Rejected because:** This recreates framework lock-in. The execution model would differ across frameworks, preventing cross-framework workflow portability and making Anvil's behavior unpredictable. Framework-controlled execution also tends to couple orchestration logic to framework-specific life"}